      "type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Constraint function",
      "sourceSha256": "a31e901940949032a17631ccc5743fe3a50298ba4d3921642221b2cccf8a2d0d",
      "outputs": [],
      "outputMimeTypes": [],
      "attachmentNames": [],
      "math": []
    },
    {
      "sourceRef": "index:1",
      "cellIndex": 1,
      "originalCellId": null,
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": "[![DIIT - UNLaM]([embedded image omitted])](https://ingenieria.unlam.edu.ar/)    \n[![by-nc-sa_80x15.png]([embedded image omitted])](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/)\n2025 [Mariano Real](mailto:mreal@unlam.edu.ar) | [Víctor Bettachini](mailto:vbettachini@unlam.edu.ar)  \n\n---",
      "sourceSha256": "0eb4747c97fe6cb3eec020537928bbafcdf41d33cc2c6d8cc2e6dbec5b88cb82",
      "outputs": [],
      "outputMimeTypes": [],
      "attachmentNames": [],
      "math": []
    },
    {
      "sourceRef": "index:2",
      "cellIndex": 2,
      "originalCellId": null,
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": "In many mechanical and physical problems we will have some relations between components that are called __constraints__.\n\nAs an example, let's work the problem shown in the following figure.",
      "sourceSha256": "3fdbede943365448e4cd573117f3481481d60178116e7c1836cd82e2920620ac",
      "outputs": [],
      "outputMimeTypes": [],
      "attachmentNames": [],
      "math": []
    },
    {
      "sourceRef": "index:3",
      "cellIndex": 3,
      "originalCellId": null,
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": "![imagen.png]([embedded image omitted])",
      "sourceSha256": "e732a7777af1b5800adf30c53dcfd92bcfb4094e03b6a2dc28f39e8d5e5556b5",
      "outputs": [],
      "outputMimeTypes": [],
      "attachmentNames": [],
      "math": []
    },
    {
      "sourceRef": "index:4",
      "cellIndex": 4,
      "originalCellId": null,
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": "We have two objects over a table (so weight and normal force are not needed for the solution), linked together through an ideal string (massles and inextensible) that goes around an ideal pulley. This means that the pulley just bends the string. The string's length is $\\ell$.\n\nIf we pull from $m_1$ towards the right, then $m_2$ must move down with the same velocity and acceleration than that of $m_1$.\n\nWe are saying that the coordinate of $m_1$ ($x_1$) and the coordinate of $m_2$ ($y_2$) must add up to the string's length:\n\n$$\nx_1 + y_2 = \\ell   \\tag{1}\n$$   \n\nWe could also differentiate this equation with respect to time to find relations for the velocities and for accelerations (the string's length is constant so its derivative is zero):\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dot{x}_1 + \\dot{y}_2 &= 0 \\\\\n\\ddot{x}_1 + \\ddot{y}_2 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n\nIf we solve this problem manually, we would use the previous equations to substitute one of the accelerations into Newton's equations.\n\nSince we are now trying to use the computer and Euler-Lagrange formalism, then we have to write the constraint (eq. 1), in a way that we can include it in this new language used by the PC and by Euler-Lagrange.\n\nWe are always going to write this equations in the form $f(z_1,z_2,z_3,...) = 0$, that is, an equation (function $f$) set equal to zero (homogeneous). The $z_i$ will be the different coordinates that we want to link, in this case $x_1,y_2$, so equation 1 now is\n\n$$\nf(x_1,y_2) = x_1 + y_2 -\\ell = 0\n$$\n",
      "sourceSha256": "3c9e6a1f68b5035992f0e3001e8b21a4e68d263ffce870c6e6d2efdd266d40f4",
      "outputs": [],
      "outputMimeTypes": [],
      "attachmentNames": [],
      "math": [
        {
          "kind": "inline",
          "latex": "\\ell"
        },
        {
          "kind": "inline",
          "latex": "m_1"
        },
        {
          "kind": "inline",
          "latex": "m_2"
        },
        {
          "kind": "inline",
          "latex": "m_1"
        },
        {
          "kind": "inline",
          "latex": "m_1"
        },
        {
          "kind": "inline",
          "latex": "x_1"
        },
        {
          "kind": "inline",
          "latex": "m_2"
        },
        {
          "kind": "inline",
          "latex": "y_2"
        },
        {
          "kind": "display",
          "latex": "\nx_1 + y_2 = \\ell   \\tag{1}\n"
        },
        {
          "kind": "inline",
          "latex": "f(z_1,z_2,z_3,...) = 0"
        },
        {
          "kind": "inline",
          "latex": "f"
        },
        {
          "kind": "inline",
          "latex": "z_i"
        },
        {
          "kind": "inline",
          "latex": "x_1,y_2"
        },
        {
          "kind": "display",
          "latex": "\nf(x_1,y_2) = x_1 + y_2 -\\ell = 0\n"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sourceRef": "index:5",
      "cellIndex": 5,
      "originalCellId": null,
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": "Next is a sample exercise with an Atwood machine, where you will find a full solution that shows how to use the constraint equation.",
      "sourceSha256": "cdd7ce1698578c77d4dbe5e3a9e488c0fe3b3bed4c686d80f8aed5615b8cabde",
      "outputs": [],
      "outputMimeTypes": [],
      "attachmentNames": [],
      "math": []
    },
    {
      "sourceRef": "index:6",
      "cellIndex": 6,
      "originalCellId": null,
      "type": "code",
      "source": "import sympy as sm # import functions for symbolic manipulations\nfrom sympy.physics import mechanics as me # from sympy we use functions for mechanics\nme.init_vprinting() # dot notation for velocity and acceleration",
      "sourceSha256": "47cc9fd83cffe716d4bbead70b9c75481d194a8ce5f3eebc74e2dc4cf9fab241",
      "outputs": [],
      "outputMimeTypes": [],
      "attachmentNames": [],
      "pythonAst": {
        "imports": [
          "sympy",
          "sympy.physics"
        ],
        "calls": [
          "me.init_vprinting"
        ],
        "literalAssignments": {},
        "parseError": null
      }
    },
    {
      "sourceRef": "index:7",
      "cellIndex": 7,
      "originalCellId": null,
      "type": "code",
      "source": "m1, m2, l = sm.symbols('m_1, m_2, \\ell', positive=True) # Parameters for the system\ne = me.ReferenceFrame('e') # Cartesian frame of reference\nx1, y2 = me.dynamicsymbols('x_1, y_2')",
      "sourceSha256": "a9a0cb707b0e323d208cde46176d358d9c4c760830ce23dcd3ee25655eb15d5b",
      "outputs": [],
      "outputMimeTypes": [],
      "attachmentNames": [],
      "pythonAst": {
        "imports": [],
        "calls": [
          "me.ReferenceFrame",
          "me.dynamicsymbols",
          "sm.symbols"
        ],
        "literalAssignments": {},
        "parseError": null
      }
    },
    {
      "sourceRef": "index:8",
      "cellIndex": 8,
      "originalCellId": null,
      "type": "code",
      "source": "# Constraint:\nf1 = sm.Eq(x1 + y2 - l, 0 ) # constraint as an homogeneous function\nf1",
      "sourceSha256": "cb4ce4aa0f2bfc6dbd2f86ac43209eb3e14d9eb2a1a3e8ccd9419e342930add2",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "execute_result",
          "text/plain": "-\\ell + x₁ + y₂ = 0",
          "text/latex": "$\\displaystyle - \\ell + x_{1} + y_{2} = 0$"
        }
      ],
      "outputMimeTypes": [
        "image/png",
        "text/latex",
        "text/plain"
      ],
      "attachmentNames": [],
      "pythonAst": {
        "imports": [],
        "calls": [
          "sm.Eq"
        ],
        "literalAssignments": {},
        "parseError": null
      }
    },
    {
      "sourceRef": "index:9",
      "cellIndex": 9,
      "originalCellId": null,
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": "From this constraint function we can isolate a variable in terms of others. For example, we can isolate $y_2$ in terms of $x_1$:",
      "sourceSha256": "189b1ab507eca2e8bfd9ed82ce0f725a09dc0ea8bc5ddf0b06996a03b7d6959c",
      "outputs": [],
      "outputMimeTypes": [],
      "attachmentNames": [],
      "math": [
        {
          "kind": "inline",
          "latex": "y_2"
        },
        {
          "kind": "inline",
          "latex": "x_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sourceRef": "index:10",
      "cellIndex": 10,
      "originalCellId": null,
      "type": "code",
      "source": "y2_f1 = sm.solveset(f1, y2).args[0] # y2 in terms of x1\ny2_f1",
      "sourceSha256": "1858869c916bec825bd79bb810ef73f7df0a258e37ffcd838d1482a9f3a4f17f",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "execute_result",
          "text/plain": "\\ell - x₁",
          "text/latex": "$\\displaystyle \\ell - x_{1}$"
        }
      ],
      "outputMimeTypes": [
        "image/png",
        "text/latex",
        "text/plain"
      ],
      "attachmentNames": [],
      "pythonAst": {
        "imports": [],
        "calls": [
          "sm.solveset"
        ],
        "literalAssignments": {},
        "parseError": null
      }
    },
    {
      "sourceRef": "index:11",
      "cellIndex": 11,
      "originalCellId": null,
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": "The same procedure if we want the generalized velocity or acceleration.",
      "sourceSha256": "f2617e119fa13b674bed8d6b93200c722171a2a013d1f182d3b9c6902f8f2561",
      "outputs": [],
      "outputMimeTypes": [],
      "attachmentNames": [],
      "math": []
    },
    {
      "sourceRef": "index:12",
      "cellIndex": 12,
      "originalCellId": null,
      "type": "code",
      "source": "t = sm.symbols('t') # time\ny2_f1.diff(t), y2_f1.diff(t, 2) # velocity and acceleration of y2",
      "sourceSha256": "13460bd4c1242ddee9234735e9597917aacfbdf22f6b24f0b552a4ce209eb621",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "execute_result",
          "text/plain": "(-x₁̇, -x₁̈)",
          "text/latex": "$\\displaystyle \\left( - \\dot{x}_{1}, \\  - \\ddot{x}_{1}\\right)$"
        }
      ],
